        "id": "ch12_6_3-1",
        "text": "Sitting down at the table\nTables are the core of the database system. They are what hold your data, and\nallow you to quickly access the information. It\u2019s a good idea to understand\nhow tables are organized in Base, and how you can use them to hold and display\nyour data.\nTo create a new table, you can either use the Base Table Wizard if you think you\nhave a standard table, or you can just create your own table in Design View.\nFigure 12-9 shows a table being created in the Table Design View dialog box.\nEach row in the Table Design represents a data field in the table. Each field must\nhave a unique Field Name and be assigned a Field Type. The Field Type defines the\ntype of data present in the field (characters, numbers, dates, and so on). The\nDescription area allows you to write a brief description describing the meaning of\nthe data field.\nCreating a new table is easy:\n1. Click the Table icon in the Database pane.\n2. Click the New Table icon in the standard toolbar.\n3. Enter a text name for the Field Name describing the data.\n4. Select a Field Type from the drop-down menu.\n5. Enter a brief description in the Description text area.\nFIGURE 12-9:\nThe Table Design\nView dialog box in\nBase.\n246   PART 3 Getting Things Done\n6. If the field is a primary key, right-click to the left of the row and select\nPrimary Key.\nA table\u2019s primary key is a data field that can uniquely identify each record in the\ntable. In the example in Figure 12-9, each employee is identified by a unique\nemployee ID because more than one employee can have the same last name.\nThe key icon appears for the field you select as the primary key.\n7. Enter the remaining rows to complete the data elements in the table.\n8. Choose File \u00c1 Save As to save the table, using a unique table name.\nThe new table now appears on the main Base window under the Tables area.\nDouble-clicking the new table brings up the Data Viewing and Entry window, as\nshown in Figure 12-10.\nIf you\u2019re familiar with Microsoft Access, you should feel right at home here. This",
